  "question": {
    "text": "A simple pendulum made of a bob of mass m and a metallic wire of a negligible mass has a time period of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>&nbsp;at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>. If the temperature of the wire is increased, and the corresponding change in its time period is plotted against its temperature, the resulting graph is a line of slope&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>. If the coefficient of linear expansion of metal is <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></math>, then the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>&nbsp;is",
    "image": null
